{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The problem was in `build_system()` at bot.py:130 — the static `INSTRUCTIONS` and the per-athlete context (which contains the long-term facts) were concatenated into **one** cache-marked block. Since prompt caching is prefix-based, any `save_fact` changed the block and invalidated the whole cache entry, so the next call re-created ~1000 tokens of instructions — exactly the `cache_read: 0` / `cache_creation: ~1000` pattern in `logs/usage.jsonl` after every `save_fact`.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- `bot.py` — `build_system()` now returns two `cache_control`-marked blocks: the static instructions in one, the dynamic athlete context in the other. A memory write only invalidates the small context block; the instructions stay in cache. The joined text is byte-identical to the old single block, so the model sees the same prompt. Also fixed the now-stale \"One block, cached as a whole\" comment.\n- `README.md` — updated the caching sentence to describe the two-breakpoint setup.\n- `ANSWER.md` — written, with root cause, change, and verification notes.\n\n**Verified** (no test suite exists in the repo, so I ran a direct script): the system prompt is two cache-marked blocks equal in content to the old one; after simulating a `save_fact` the instructions block is byte-identical while only the context block changes; an unchanged record rebuilds identical blocks. One thing I couldn't verify: a live API call (no `COACH_ANTHROPIC_KEY` here) — the effect should appear in `logs/usage.jsonl` as `cache_read_input_tokens` remaining high on rounds after a `save_fact` instead of dropping to 0."}
